Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Carrier Tone Beneath the Seventh String
- Story & Context: During a late test, Nia hears a faint AM carrier whistle just below one guqin harmonic. Lin sustains the note and slowly shifts left-hand pressure until the beating between string and receiver becomes audible. Instead of tuning the electronics, the band preserves the mismatch. The track begins with dry guqin harmonics and one clipped kick cell. Wei answers every second phrase with a felt-damped marimba note, while Marcus holds a bass-trumpet tone below the perceived root and rises into alignment only after sixteen bars. Halfway through, the receiver is switched off but the ensemble keeps phrasing around the absent tone. The final section restores the carrier for only three seconds before cutting to silence.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Dark guqin strings in extreme foreground beside an old AM receiver with a narrow tuning needle, faint red indicator lamp, graphite frequency note reading “7,” prepared-marimba mallet on a paper chart, cup-muted bass trumpet in soft focus. Spoken fragments limited to “hold seven,” “leave the carrier,” “do not retune,” and “cut now.”

Track 2: Marimba Bar Wrapped in Frequency Paper
- Story & Context: Wei finds an obsolete printed frequency chart and wraps a thin strip around one marimba bar, creating a papery buzz after each strike. Nia maps that imperfect attack into a two-bar Baltimore club pattern without sampling it, forcing Wei to perform the mechanical timing live. Lin responds with short guqin slides that end just before the buzz decays, while Marcus plays breath-heavy bass-trumpet stabs only on the missing drum hits. In the bridge, the paper strip is removed and the groove suddenly feels too clean. The final sixteen bars restore the wrapped bar, making its defect function like the hook.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Prepared marimba bar wrapped with a narrow strip of yellowed radio frequency paper, pencil numbers visible, guqin bridge and silk-like strings nearby, bass-trumpet cup mute on a gray console, dry institutional task light. Spoken fragments limited to “wrap that bar,” “leave the buzz,” “too clean,” and “put the paper back.”

Track 3: Relay Room Forgets the Downbeat
- Story & Context: An old relay panel begins clicking irregularly during rehearsal, repeatedly landing just before or after the expected first beat. Nia removes the programmed downbeat from every fourth phrase and lets the relay become the only cue. Lin plays a descending guqin glissando across each missing entrance, making the absence feel intentional rather than broken. Wei uses muted wooden-frame taps while Marcus sustains one low brass note through the entire gap. Near the end, the relay stops on its own. The band preserves the missing downbeat for two more cycles, proving that the error has already become composition. The final sound is a dry guqin finger release after the drums have ended.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Old gray-green relay cabinet with one small red lamp and handwritten graphite arrows, guqin player reflected faintly in the metal door, prepared-marimba frame at one side, bass-trumpet bell catching a narrow strip of light. Spoken fragments limited to “where is one,” “leave it missing,” “two more cycles,” and “release after the cut.”

